th with fda  and we also have established a strategy  on engaging with  regulators outside of united states um  and we believe again as i mentioned  before right that engaging with  regulators at these earlier stages  um is is imperative uh to advance this  field  um of with these technologies and  we have received an awesome response uh  for from fda  the interaction has been incredible  there  um and back and forth discussions and we  continue to have  frequent teleconferences between these  engagement  so we really believe that again  this collaborative interaction will help  us  understand these technologies and move  these technologies and advance them  so what are our deliverables so so far  as i mentioned in february 2020  we had a joint workshop with  fda and iqmps affiliate and this was at  fda location we also  agreed to develop the post workshop  publication  where we will discuss the outcomes or  what happened  at the workshop and our perspective on  these technologies  and we also worked with fda to provide  us with  questions which we could incorporate  into  the survey 2.0 so they had a chance to  look at that we actually presented some  of the survey results at this workshop  that we had with fda  and now having incorporated some of the  questions  that were shared with us through fda um  it just  gonna really help us better understand  um what they are looking for  um in this survey and then we also have  initiated  webinar series um so between these  workshops the face-to-face workshops we  really want to continue our engagement  so we had so far two webinars where you  have representatives from  iqmps affiliate nfda and addressing  various topics  and presentations but we do spend quite  a bit of time  with q a and discussions  which have been very productive  so as i mentioned you know we have a  communication and external organization  outreach uh group um and  it's imperative for us to to to have  this group  because one to communicate all of the  things that we're doing  and having a comprehensive communication  strategy  even just within the iq but obviously  also  externally and you know how to engage  with non-regulatory stakeholders  right so as the field is expanding and  growing there's a lot of other  organizations that are engaging or  working in this area so again  we don't want to duplicate efforts so  this group is looking  at other stakeholders and engaging with  them  to see you know can we collaborate  together or  share information so some of the  deliverables  this group has launched an awesome  website iqmpsaffiliate.org again that's  where you can find information about  us but also different projects that  we're working on  and also having access to the  publications that i mentioned earlier  this group has also implemented a social  media strategy  so for example you can see us on  linkedin um and you can join us there  and see updates from this group one of  the  things that you know as a camp is  affiliate very often we're asked to  participate and present at different  events conferences or workshops so  each time we had to develop you know  powerpoint slides or presentations for  each one of those  so this was pretty intense process so  this group helped us develop  a presentation materials which we can  utilize to communicate  our accomplishments and our efforts and  obviously  that result from that is what you're  seeing even today  that i'm presenting and we also want to  raise awareness  with the key external stakeholders  regarding us as an iqmp as affiliate  um but even more importantly about these  technologies  and where pharmaceutical industry sees  these technologies  moving forward  so our project proposals team is also  very busy and certainly  very excited excited team  so there's three areas here where this  project team is working on  so one looking at ability to share  data retrospective data so  various members of iqmbps affiliate  and obviously i have been working with  these technologies  so it's an opportunity for us in a  pre-competitive environment to share  the data um right and where to share it  and how to do it  who would have access to it as you can  imagine that's not an easy proposition  but we are certainly working on it um  and the goal  for this specific project is to develop  a mechanism in a framework  to share collate and analyze existing  nps data the milestones here  we have initiated a call for  participants to join  this discussion and we are focusing on  specific organs or context of use  the other objective here  is for joint characterization right so  identify  a specific organ and be able to  characterize it  so here we have actually made quite a  bit of progress i shouldn't say actually  we are making quite a bit of progress  the first area of interest was a gi  mps and then second kidney mps and you  can actually go  on our website and see an rfi  and rfp for the gi  mps which enable  prospective and collaborative projects  to characterize  these two platforms the gi and and the  kidney one  the kidney one uh we also have engaged  with psdc  kidney biomarkers team and again this  came through that  other working group the collaborative  collaborative  group that i mentioned before the  suggestion from there were engaged  with the pstc and that we identified  this is one  area where we could collaborate so again  so we're not duplicating efforts  and then the third project  is a category is prospective  collaborations  so here the goal is to identify  collaboration and alignment  opportunities  for ongoing projects within our  individual companies  so we have initiated a call for  participants  to join these groups and discussions  and we're going to be focusing on  specific organs and context of use  we have not defined those which wasn't  prioritized as yet  but we're getting there  so the other big question really comes  up  when we think about these technologies  is which species  so should we engage with human  cell-based mps or animal cell based mps  and a lot of efforts until now  have really concentrated on a human cell  based mps  and of course you know when you think  about it it does make sense  right we're trying to uh translate  our medicines from pre-clinical  to clinics we want to make sure that  those medicines are safe  and effective for our patients we're  trying to do it  at a faster pace if at all possible so  working with  human cell based mps for human medicines  makes sense but there's certainly some  areas where  the animal cell based mps can be helpful  and so here obviously i'm going to  address  in the context of drug discovery and  development  but there are also other areas there was  just a workshop  sponsored by national academy of  sciences where animal cell based  technologies were discussed in the  context of for example veterinary  medicine  or agro chemical industry  so there are certainly other areas where  these technologies are also  playing a significant role but so let's  step back  and talk about you know these two  aspects within drug discovery and  development  so we have come together and have  developed  a position statement on  utilization of animal cell based  microphysical systems  and here i'll just mention them as  animal mps  so we certainly are recognizing the  importance and the impact  and the need for animal models we  certainly would not be able to  provide all of the medicines that we're  currently are providing to  our patients without in vivo models  but there are certainly some areas where  we can refine where there's for example  lack of those models or the models  are not as helpful as they potentially  could be  so we are recognizing you know there's a  new approach  new methodologies that we couldn't that  could enhance  this drug discovery and development path  so exclusive utilization of human  cell-based systems  if we that's the direction we're going  to go and it makes it really challenging  to support the investigation of  species-specific mechanisms  demonstrate the models recapitulate his  topological findings where we have  significant amount of data  from our pre-clinical in vivo studies  and validate predictivity for toxicities  that only occur above relevant clinical  exposures in humans  right so there's only up to a certain  expo  a certain level that we can expose  folks they're part of clinical trials so  if we want to go beyond that  obviously we're not able to do that so  animal mps systems  could help us in this specific area  so this is where again we're suggesting  that  mps composed of cells from other species  can help us advance microphysiological  systems  and their use  so with that  with supporting utilization of animal  cell-based systems you know where else  can they help us  right so for example cross-referencing  animal cell-based mps data  with enormous amount of in-vivo animal  data that we already have  to accelerate the development and  validation of novel mps technologies  right there's not we have human  cell data but not certainly to the  extent that we have in vivo  the amount that we have in of in vivo  animal data  we can also leverage animal and vivo  data with animal mps data to gain  confidence  and productivity of human  microphysiological systems  and also identifying species-specific  differences  for selection of translating models  through mps species comparison  and especially for the rare diseases  developing transgenic and  disease-specific mps models  with cells derived from transgenic mice  and rats as we know the transgenic  in vivo models have been enormously  helpful  and evolving and developing various  medicines for our patients  so here utilizing the cells  transgenic or modified genetically  modified cells  can be very helpful a carrying out  structure activity relationship  or sar and mechanistic studies in  instances  where there are non-clinical toxicology  issues they need resolution so for  